{"id": "live-387", "title": "Bluetriton Brands, Inc. v. Washington Township Zoning Hearing Board and Weis Markets, Inc. (Pennsylvania Court Splits on Shopping Center Gas Station Fight)", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "appeal", "date": "2026-11-10", "jurisdiction": "Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania", "state": "PA", "amount": "No dollar figure stated; dispute over adding a fuel station to a 20.3-acre shopping center", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11058438/bluetriton-brands-inc-v-washington-twp-zhb-and-weis-markets-inc/", "summary": "The Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania reversed a Northampton County trial court's holding that Weis Markets needed to submit a site plan before adding a fuel station to its Bangor Plaza supermarket, but remanded the case because the trial court never addressed all of objector Bluetriton Brands' arguments against the Zoning Hearing Board's grant of a special exception for the project. Bluetriton, which operates a spring-water extraction business on parcels adjoining and downstream of the site, presented hydrogeology and petroleum-storage expert testimony that the fuel station could contaminate the aquifer feeding its Deer Park brand's Greenwaltz Spring source.", "significance": "The decision shows that a favorable zoning board approval does not insulate a shopping-center use expansion from a well-resourced neighboring business's environmental objection, and that an ambiguous local ordinance can leave a site-plan-versus-special-exception approval unresolved for years even after an appellate win on one issue. It is also a reminder that a chain's compliance history at other locations can become part of the evidentiary record on whether a proposed use poses a genuine risk at a new site.", "judge": "Ren\u00e9e Cohn Jubelirer", "tags": ["zoning", "special-exception", "shopping-center", "groundwater", "pennsylvania"]}